{"working":"The line starts with a clef and no key signature. Next comes a sharp before three small notes: a 16th pair and then an 8th. These are the G#4–A4 16ths and the A4 8th from late in bar 85. Then a 16th rest and a beamed group of three 16ths: B4, then B4–C5 under a slur. That is the rest 16th, B4 16th staccato and (B4 C5) that close bar 85. A barline follows. So the line begins inside bar 85, with only its last few notes. The tail of bar 85 sits at the start of this line rather than the end of the previous one.

After the barline there is an 'f' above the staff. Then come four dotted figures, each a 32nd plus a dotted 16th: C5–G4, A4–F4, E4–G4, and B3–D4 low on the staff. These are the four groups that open bar 86. Next come a low C4 8th and a stacked chord written with strokes (the two C5+E4+G3 8ths). Then the repeat sign. These are bar 86.

After the repeat there is a barline and a curved mark. It looks like the start of the next section or a custos, and it holds no notes I can match to the edition. One mismatch: the copyist puts the 'f' at the start of bar 86, but the edition puts it on the C4 8th. In short, the line runs from late bar 85 to bar 86.","right":false,"insights":"The opening fragment is short: a sharp, a G#–A pair, a rest and a B–C slur before the first barline. A matcher that only looks for whole bars can skip it, and the line's start then gets pushed to bar 86. The four dotted 32nd figures and the repeat sign make bar 86 easy to find. Matching the line's first notes against the end of the previous bar would have shown the line begins in bar 85."}
